The page links four kill chains: two in the same infostealer economy, one that used the OIDC token a week earlier to publish npm packages, and one as a contrast.
notes:
Four related kill chains on csoh.org, and the page's related defense topics.

At stage 01 the page says the same infostealer economy sits under UNC5537 / Snowflake and under the vendor endpoint that started the Vercel / Context.ai chain.

At stage 06 it says the OIDC token is what Mini Shai-Hulud used a week earlier to publish npm packages carrying valid provenance.

At stage 07 it compares this campaign with tj-actions/changed-files, which dumped secrets into publicly readable build logs and needed no network path at all.

The four defense topics the page links are CI/CD security, GitHub Actions, non-human identity and detection engineering. CI/CD stands for continuous integration and continuous delivery.
